{"name":"Harbour Fog","hex":"#afb1b4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"harbour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Standard spelling of harbor."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Standard spelling of harbor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/harbour"},{"term":"fog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick cloud that forms near the ground; the obscurity of such a cloud.","A mist or film clouding a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with or as if with fog.","To become obscured in condensation or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Harbor","blocks":[{"text":"A harbor or harbour is a sheltered part of a body of water where ships, boats, and barges can be moored."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Jebel_Ali_Port_2_Imresolt.jpg/330px-Jebel_Ali_Port_2_Imresolt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harbor","wikidata":"Q283202"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog","wikidata":"Q37477"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c